# `reporting/` — Papers, slides, reports

Where results become communication. Unlike `results/` (machine-made, regenerable),
everything here is **human-authored, point-in-time, and versioned along its own
lifecycle**.

```
reporting/
├── _assets/         # shared: lab.cls, refs.bib, theme-colors.tex (GENERATED)
├── papers/          # manuscripts + frozen submission snapshots
├── posters/         # conference posters (large-format)
├── presentations/   # dated slide decks
└── writeups/        # periodic internal reports & updates
```

Each is a separate subfolder because the artifact types have different lifecycles
and audiences — a manuscript accretes over months, a poster or deck is a dated
one-off tied to a venue, a report is periodic. They *share* figures and style, but
they are not the same thing.

## Rules that apply to everything here
1. **Reference figures, don't copy them.** Point at `../results/figures/light/…`
   (papers) or `…/dark/…` (dark decks). The exception is frozen snapshots (below),
   where copying *is* the point.
2. **Record provenance.** Each artifact notes the git SHA / {{ data_versioning }}
   rev of the results it was built from, so "which version did I show?" is
   answerable later.
3. **Shared style, once.** Article PDFs (paper/poster/writeup) build from
   `_assets/lab.cls` + `_assets/refs.bib`; the Beamer deck brings its own class but
   shares `_assets/fonts.tex` + the theme colours. `_assets/theme-colors.tex` is
   **generated** from `conf/theme/` — do not edit it by hand.
4. **A human commits the words.** Agents may draft prose and wire figure
   references; people own the text. (See `AGENTS.md`.)

Per-type conventions: [`papers/`](papers/README.md),
[`posters/`](posters/README.md), [`presentations/`](presentations/README.md),
[`writeups/`](writeups/README.md).
